 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; the server commits each edit as one event revision and pushes committed revisions to browsers over WebSocket connections that the project operates itself. Browsers display a revisioned view of the board and apply pushed revisions in revision order.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery is push-based, so change storage and browser recovery follow from it. Revisions are kept in a replayable sequence, so a browser that reconnects can catch up from its last applied revision. Publication of committed revisions to connected sockets is reliable, so a connected browser does not silently skip a revision. The project accepts the operational work this brings: owning connection capacity, session lifecycle, and reconnect handling. In exchange, the board stays off any realtime vendor's pricing and provider coupling.
+
+A browser whose connection is down shows that state; it never presents a stale board as current.
 
